WEBVTT

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.982
There is a novel, published in 1872, by the French writer Jules Verne: Around the World

00:00:12.982 --> 00:00:14.842
in Eighty Days.

00:00:14.853 --> 00:00:20.608
Its hero is Phileas Fogg, a London gentleman who runs his life by the clock.

00:00:20.616 --> 00:00:25.555
Fogg bets half his fortune that he can circle the whole world in eighty days.

00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:32.726
And tonight — Wednesday the second of October, 1872 — at a quarter to nine, he leaves.

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:36.266
We are travelling his route, starting today.

00:00:36.274 --> 00:00:39.397
Look at the map: only London is lit.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:43.976
Day by day, we'll light the rest, one port at a time.

00:00:43.984 --> 00:00:46.568
One name before we start.

00:00:46.576 --> 00:00:52.700
This morning Fogg hired a French valet, Passepartout, whose watch runs four minutes slow.

00:00:52.708 --> 00:00:54.739
Remember the watch.

00:00:54.747 --> 00:00:58.582
Now to the street where it all begins: Savile Row.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.659
Here we are: Savile Row, a quiet street in Mayfair.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.307
This is where the novel begins.

00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:13.762
Phileas Fogg lives on this street — at No. 7, Verne tells us — and from that door he leaves

00:01:13.762 --> 00:01:16.359
tonight to go round the world.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:22.675
Look in the windows as we walk: bolts of cloth, chalk marks, half-made jackets.

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:27.938
This was already tailoring country in 1872, and it still is.

00:01:27.946 --> 00:01:35.991
Henry Poole put his front door on the Row in 1846 and is still here — since 1982 at No. 15.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:38.688
Now, about No. 7.

00:01:38.696 --> 00:01:44.978
Verne picked a famous address: 'the house in which Sheridan died in 1814', he wrote.

00:01:44.986 --> 00:01:51.702
In fact Sheridan, the playwright, lived at No. 14 — and died there in 1816.

00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:54.952
The plaque on No. 14 remembers him.

00:01:54.959 --> 00:01:59.109
There's no plaque for Fogg anywhere; he lived only in the novel.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.429
Fogg's day, by the clock.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.656
He rises at eight.

00:02:06.664 --> 00:02:10.129
Tea and toast at twenty-three minutes past.

00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:16.116
Shaving-water at nine thirty-seven, at thirty degrees — eighty-six Fahrenheit.

00:02:16.124 --> 00:02:20.195
Out of the door at half past eleven, home at midnight.

00:02:20.203 --> 00:02:25.861
Into this house, at twenty-nine minutes past eleven, walks Passepartout: a Parisian of

00:02:25.861 --> 00:02:28.314
Paris, about thirty.

00:02:28.322 --> 00:02:30.708
He wants a quiet life.

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:36.024
Look at his face on the card; we like him already — the man with the slow watch.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.771
From Savile Row, Fogg walks to his club and counts.

00:02:42.781 --> 00:02:47.307
Five hundred and seventy-five steps with the right foot, five hundred and seventy-six

00:02:47.307 --> 00:02:48.905
with the left.

00:02:48.913 --> 00:02:52.089
One thousand one hundred and fifty-one.

00:02:52.097 --> 00:02:54.221
He never hurries.

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:58.615
We are on Pall Mall now, walking west through clubland.

00:02:58.623 --> 00:03:03.931
It was one of the first streets in London lit by gas, in 1807.

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:10.484
Watch the left side: the Athenaeum at 107, then the Travellers at 106.

00:03:10.492 --> 00:03:16.873
Then the palazzo with the tall windows — the Reform Club, 104 Pall Mall, by Charles Barry,

00:03:16.873 --> 00:03:19.378
opened 1841.

00:03:19.386 --> 00:03:23.734
Fogg made it 1,151 steps.

00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:26.247
We make it about a kilometre.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:35.957
The Reform Club is real, and still here: 104 Pall Mall, since 1836.

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:41.678
In the novel this is Fogg's whole world: he eats here, reads here, plays cards here, every

00:03:41.678 --> 00:03:43.842
day until midnight.

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:50.836
Charles Barry's palazzo opened in 1841: Portland stone outside; inside, a saloon two storeys

00:03:50.836 --> 00:03:53.302
high under a glass roof.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:57.605
And the famous kitchens that the chef Alexis Soyer helped to plan.

00:03:57.613 --> 00:04:00.999
Now Fogg's breakfast, taken here.

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:05.131
A broiled fish with Reading sauce — keep that in mind.

00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:11.289
Roast beef, a tart, Cheshire cheese, and the tea the Reform is famous for.

00:04:11.297 --> 00:04:14.421
The front today; Barry's elevation.

00:04:14.428 --> 00:04:20.644
And here, the saloon engraved in 1841 — no camera has been inside for us.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.647
Verne describes the club in loving detail.

00:04:26.657 --> 00:04:28.834
Mosaic floors.

00:04:28.842 --> 00:04:33.755
A gallery under a dome, held up by twenty red porphyry columns.

00:04:33.763 --> 00:04:36.163
Blue painted windows.

00:04:36.171 --> 00:04:41.136
A dining-room with nine windows onto a garden already gilded with autumn.

00:04:41.144 --> 00:04:43.913
Which of these details is real?

00:04:43.921 --> 00:04:47.570
A dining-room with nine windows onto the garden.

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:49.215
Exact.

00:04:49.223 --> 00:04:54.899
Nine tall casement windows in the coffee-room's south wall, and the garden is still there.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.005
Evening, at the club.

00:05:00.016 --> 00:05:04.982
Fogg and five friends sit down to whist, the card game they play nightly.

00:05:04.989 --> 00:05:09.595
In the novel — only in the novel — a thief has robbed the Bank of England of fifty-five

00:05:09.595 --> 00:05:11.587
thousand pounds.

00:05:11.595 --> 00:05:16.305
The Daily Telegraph newspaper has done the sums: the world can now be crossed in eighty

00:05:16.305 --> 00:05:17.758
days.

00:05:17.766 --> 00:05:20.297
That talk becomes a wager.

00:05:20.305 --> 00:05:25.416
Andrew Stuart, an engineer, bets four thousand pounds it can't be done.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:31.455
Fogg stakes twenty thousand — half his fortune — that it can: 'eighty days or less'.

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:36.232
Look at the card: six names, Stuart first, Fogg last.

00:05:36.239 --> 00:05:41.832
Due back 'in this very room of the Reform Club, on Saturday, the 21st of December, at

00:05:41.832 --> 00:05:47.561
a quarter before nine p.m.' Exactly eighty days — and it really was a Saturday.

00:05:47.568 --> 00:05:51.442
Fogg's motto: 'The unforeseen does not exist.'

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:56.034
Fogg's bank, Baring's, fell in 1995.

00:05:56.042 --> 00:05:58.692
The wager has outlived the bank.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.964
How could anyone even think it?

00:06:03.974 --> 00:06:06.598
Because the world had just shrunk.

00:06:06.606 --> 00:06:11.343
When one of the card players said the world was big, Fogg's answer was three words: '

00:06:11.343 --> 00:06:13.111
It was once.'

00:06:13.119 --> 00:06:15.440
Look at the three lights.

00:06:15.448 --> 00:06:19.966
The Suez Canal, opened in November 1869.

00:06:19.974 --> 00:06:25.479
The American railroad, joined at Promontory Summit in May 1869.

00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:31.177
The last rail link across India, closed at Jabalpur in March 1870.

00:06:31.185 --> 00:06:36.453
Three doors, opened within ten months, two years before tonight.

00:06:36.461 --> 00:06:38.808
Now watch the line draw.

00:06:38.816 --> 00:06:45.190
London to Suez, by rail and steamer through Mont Cenis and Brindisi, seven days.

00:06:45.198 --> 00:06:48.782
Suez to Bombay by steamer, thirteen.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.308
Bombay to Calcutta by rail, three.

00:06:52.316 --> 00:06:55.506
Calcutta to Hong Kong, thirteen.

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:58.690
Hong Kong to Yokohama, six.

00:06:58.698 --> 00:07:02.598
Yokohama to San Francisco, twenty-two.

00:07:02.606 --> 00:07:06.703
San Francisco to New York by rail, seven.

00:07:06.711 --> 00:07:09.782
New York back to London, nine.

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.953
Add them up: eighty.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.046
Twenty-five past seven.

00:07:17.056 --> 00:07:22.517
Fogg goes home from the club, calls Passepartout and says: 'We start for Dover and Calais

00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:24.391
in ten minutes.'

00:07:24.398 --> 00:07:29.706
Dover, on the English coast; Calais, across the water in France.

00:07:29.714 --> 00:07:35.772
Where to? 'We are going round the world.' 'In eighty days.'

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.943
Passepartout, remember, wanted a quiet life.

00:07:39.951 --> 00:07:43.614
One carpet-bag, and it must hold everything.

00:07:43.622 --> 00:07:47.851
Verne gives the list; ours keeps the six things that matter.

00:07:47.859 --> 00:07:51.417
Two shirts and three pairs of stockings each.

00:07:51.425 --> 00:07:54.575
A mackintosh, a travelling-cloak.

00:07:54.583 --> 00:08:00.693
The red-bound Bradshaw — the world's timetable, hundreds of pages of trains and hotels.

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:05.601
And a goodly roll of Bank of England notes: twenty thousand pounds.

00:08:05.609 --> 00:08:09.048
No books, because there are none in the house.

00:08:09.056 --> 00:08:12.035
The electric clock stays on the mantel.

00:08:12.043 --> 00:08:16.325
And so, tonight, does the gas — remember that.

00:08:16.333 --> 00:08:21.075
Two shirts, three pairs of stockings, and half a fortune.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:30.113
Down to the end of Savile Row and into a cab — a hansom, most likely; Verne never says.

00:08:30.124 --> 00:08:32.997
To the cabby, Fogg is a swell.

00:08:33.005 --> 00:08:36.550
The fare is sixpence a mile or part of one.

00:08:36.558 --> 00:08:42.945
Watch the line on the 1872 map, south and east to Trafalgar Square and the Strand.

00:08:42.953 --> 00:08:48.432
London already had an Underground — nine years old, steam-hauled and smoky.

00:08:48.439 --> 00:08:50.524
Fogg takes a cab.

00:08:50.532 --> 00:08:56.479
Listen: hooves on stone, and a galop by Offenbach — French dance music — because Passepartout

00:08:56.479 --> 00:08:58.260
is a Parisian.

00:08:58.268 --> 00:09:01.787
They left the club at twenty-five past seven.

00:09:01.795 --> 00:09:05.221
They are at Charing Cross at twenty past eight.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.102
The Strand, and ahead, the forecourt of Charing Cross.

00:09:12.113 --> 00:09:16.460
Forget olde-worlde London: this was Fogg's newest London.

00:09:16.468 --> 00:09:21.828
The station had opened in January 1864 — eight years old that night.

00:09:21.836 --> 00:09:26.289
The hotel behind the stone cross, 1865.

00:09:26.297 --> 00:09:29.815
The cross itself, 1865.

00:09:29.823 --> 00:09:32.921
The Underground, nine years old.

00:09:32.928 --> 00:09:35.947
The Embankment by the river, two.

00:09:35.955 --> 00:09:39.434
Big Ben — the clock tower — thirteen.

00:09:39.442 --> 00:09:43.618
So much of what Fogg passes tonight is younger than his valet.

00:09:43.626 --> 00:09:50.547
You can't see it from here: the station's great roof — one iron span, 155 metres, five

00:09:50.547 --> 00:09:52.276
hundred feet.

00:09:52.284 --> 00:09:57.065
It fell in 1905 and was replaced; that's all we'll say.

00:09:57.073 --> 00:09:59.907
Then the one warm beat of the evening.

00:09:59.915 --> 00:10:03.644
At the station a barefoot woman with a child asks for money.

00:10:03.652 --> 00:10:07.881
Fogg hands her the twenty guineas he has just won at whist.

00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:10.328
Without breaking stride.

00:10:10.336 --> 00:10:15.368
Four hundred and twenty shillings — about eight hundred and forty cab-miles.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:24.033
On the left, an engraving from 1872 — the year itself — the hotel and forecourt as Fogg

00:10:24.033 --> 00:10:25.512
saw them.

00:10:25.522 --> 00:10:28.435
On the right, the cross today.

00:10:28.443 --> 00:10:33.093
Six platforms behind it, and still about a train an hour to Dover.

00:10:33.101 --> 00:10:36.935
The hotel is a hotel still, under a newer name.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:42.783
Twenty to nine, still under the roof.

00:10:42.793 --> 00:10:46.062
Look up — what is the weather doing tonight?

00:10:46.069 --> 00:10:49.483
A dark night and a fine, steady rain.

00:10:49.491 --> 00:10:51.746
Verne's own words.

00:10:51.754 --> 00:10:55.904
Rain on the station roof, then rain on the carriage window.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:01.677
It's twenty to nine, and it's raining.

00:11:01.687 --> 00:11:03.916
Who is your new master?

00:11:04.639 --> 00:11:09.691
<v Passepartout>Mr Phileas Fogg of Savile Row, monsieur — the most exact and settled gentleman in the

00:11:09.691 --> 00:11:12.512
<v Passepartout>United Kingdom, they told me.

00:11:12.523 --> 00:11:19.423
<v Passepartout>Rises at eight, tea and toast at twenty-three past, out at half past eleven, home at midnight.

00:11:19.431 --> 00:11:21.304
<v Passepartout>A real machine.

00:11:21.312 --> 00:11:24.147
<v Passepartout>I do not mind serving a machine.

00:11:24.154 --> 00:11:27.870
<v Passepartout>I have known him since twenty-nine minutes past eleven.

00:11:28.612 --> 00:11:30.959
We've got a train to catch.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:38.625
A postcard from 1905, thirty-three years after Fogg.

00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:44.325
The same platforms, and — just — the old roof, months before it fell.

00:11:44.333 --> 00:11:47.285
The Paris boat train, waiting.

00:11:47.293 --> 00:11:49.575
Now, tonight.

00:11:49.583 --> 00:11:52.351
Twenty to nine, seated.

00:11:52.359 --> 00:11:57.583
A quarter to nine — the whistle, and we're off, over Hungerford Bridge, the way trains

00:11:57.583 --> 00:11:59.983
still leave Fogg's station.

00:11:59.991 --> 00:12:03.904
We travel in daylight; Fogg had rain and dark.

00:12:03.912 --> 00:12:09.425
Look right — the Embankment along the river, two years old in 1872, and beyond it the

00:12:09.425 --> 00:12:11.956
clock tower, thirteen.

00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:15.364
Brand-new London slipping away behind him.

00:12:15.372 --> 00:12:19.640
This train, today, takes about two hours to Dover.

00:12:19.648 --> 00:12:25.522
Fogg's timetable gave him seven days to reach Suez, and this is the first hour of them.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:29.825
A few miles out, Passepartout sits bolt upright.

00:12:29.833 --> 00:12:35.543
He has forgotten to turn off the gas in his room. 'It will burn,' says Fogg, 'at your

00:12:35.543 --> 00:12:37.167
expense.'

00:12:37.175 --> 00:12:39.141
To be continued.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:46.793
Your souvenir of the day: Fogg's last breakfast in London — the meal he ate before betting

00:12:46.793 --> 00:12:48.864
half his fortune.

00:12:48.875 --> 00:12:53.467
The dish is broiled fish with a Reading-sauce-style ketchup — a real Berkshire condiment

00:12:53.467 --> 00:12:57.656
from 1802, gone since the 1960s.

00:12:57.664 --> 00:13:01.498
The recipe is on the card, with the rest of the menu.

00:13:01.506 --> 00:13:05.941
Cook it on the evening of the twenty-first of December, and be at table by a quarter to

00:13:05.941 --> 00:13:07.314
nine.

00:13:07.322 --> 00:13:10.498
That's when he promised to be back in that room.

00:13:10.506 --> 00:13:16.314
Fogg's other motto, and ours for the road: 'A well-used minimum suffices for everything.'

00:13:16.322 --> 00:13:20.130
Next: Dover, Calais, Paris.
